 "asked_at": "2026-10-17T19:07+08:00",
 "answer": "Because Johor Bahru and Singapore share the same immediate geographical region separated only by the Straits of Johor, they are affected by the same regional transboundary smoke haze driven by land and forest fires in Indonesia. \n\nAir quality metrics fluctuate throughout the day based on shifting winds and localized weather patterns, but both cities experience comparable levels of haze:\n\n* **Singapore:** The National Environment Agency (NEA) reported 24-hour Pollutant Standards Index (PSI) readings mostly lingering in the **\"Unhealthy\"** range (with regional readings generally spanning between 130 and 185, and specific hourly PM2.5 spikes hitting high brackets). \n* **Johor Bahru:** Real-time air quality tracking (such as IQAir and local Department of Environment metrics) similarly places JB's air quality index in the **\"Unhealthy\" to \"Very Unhealthy\"** spectrum. \n\nBecause they are right next to each other, **neither city is drastically worse than the other**; both regions experience heavy haze episodes concurrently depending on which direction the wind blows the smoke from Sumatra or Kalimantan. You can expect poor visibility and similar hazy conditions whether you are on the Singaporean or Malaysian side of the border.",
